  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-RAS826</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A. South Asia

In South Asia, extensive discussion and preparation for the ratification of several ILO standards are ongoing, including Conventions Nos. 155, 187, and 190. Despite high interest, the capacity of trade unions to engage with normative actions remains low. The ILO has provided past technical assistance through publications and workshops, aiming to strengthen the capacity and organizational efforts of trade unions in promoting, reporting, and applying international labor standards.
The ILO&apos;s efforts aim to enhance the capacity of trade unions to effectively engage in the promotion, reporting, and application of international labor standards, ensuring better protection of workers&apos; rights and improving labor conditions in the region. For the current biennium, the ILO&apos;s strategy includes publishing and disseminating the C190 publication to promote its ratification and application. Draft briefs on Conventions Nos. 155 and 187 are being finalized and will be used for promotion. An advocacy brief on Conventions Nos. 97 and 143 will be developed. To further strengthen the capacity of trade unions, a subregional training is proposed to establish a network of activists involved in ILS promotion and application.

B. Pacific Island Countries

In Pacific Island Countries, weaknesses in existing labour law frameworks have led to poor reporting records and low levels of ratification of international labour standards. Additionally, the region suffers from extremely high rates of violence against women and girls, particularly domestic workers who often work unprotected by labour law. The ILO has provided technical assistance by prioritizing the improvement of the application of ratified international labour standards and facilitating informed dialogue around these standards.
